WEBVTT

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.360
Speaker 1: There is a new theological term that you must learn, and that is propitiation.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.640
Speaker 1: Propitiation.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.000
Speaker 1: And what is the difference?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.160
Speaker 1: Here is something you can use to remember.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.640
Speaker 1: Substitutionary atonement and propitiation are actually cause and effect.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:45.080
Speaker 1: And by that I mean that when Christ took our place and died our death, or simply put by the redemptive work of Christ, the justice demands of God was appeased.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.560
Speaker 1: And that's what propitiation is.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:55.820
Speaker 1: All right, in simple terms, it means to regain faith favor with someone with whom you lost favor.

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:56.260
Speaker 1: All right?

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.220
Speaker 1: So it means to appease.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:01.020
Speaker 1: Actually, that's what propitiation really means.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:07.740
Speaker 1: And from the flow of thought In Isaiah, chapter 53, from verse 10 to 11, you see this idea portrayed there.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:13.340
Speaker 1: So verse 10, you know, will pass as an explanation of substitutionary atonement.

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:20.150
Speaker 1: The Bible says, yet it pleased the Lord to bruise him, he put him to grief when he shall make his soul an offering for sin.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.190
Speaker 1: That substitutionary atonement, right?

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.190
Speaker 1: He shall see his seed, he shall prolong his days.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:26.070
Speaker 1: And all of that.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:27.390
Speaker 1: Then verse 11, pay attention.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:32.350
Speaker 1: Verse 11 says, he shall see the labor of his soul.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:38.270
Speaker 1: KJV says to travel the suffering of his soul and be satisfied.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.870
Speaker 1: You see, another word for propitiation is satisfaction.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:47.140
Speaker 1: When the demands of something has been met, you know, wholesomely.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.140
Speaker 1: All right, he shall see the travel of his soul and be satisfied.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:54.500
Speaker 1: Meaning the substitutionary atonement was enough.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:09.300
Speaker 1: And so now God says, well, it's just like Jesus saying, it is finished, it is done, the sacrifice has been provided, it is fulfilled, and it is sufficient.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.750
Speaker 1: All right, so that's the idea there.

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:20.590
Speaker 1: Another text that can help you understand this, especially from the Old Testament, is Zephaniah 3:17.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.470
Speaker 1: The Lord thy God in the midst of thee is mighty.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:27.390
Speaker 1: He will joy over thee, singing, he will rest in his love.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:39.230
Speaker 1: And this was actually like prophetic declaration of the redemption that is in Christ, that when the demands of justice have been met, God will rest in his love.

00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:39.790
Speaker 1: All right?

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.430
Speaker 1: He's not holding anything against us.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.230
Speaker 1: Neither can anyone hold anything against us.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:47.790
Speaker 1: All right?

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:51.630
Speaker 1: There is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:52.350
Speaker 1: All right?

00:02:53.309 --> 00:02:56.910
Speaker 1: Two New Testament texts that actually use the word propitiation.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.030
Speaker 1: I just want to share with you.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:02:59.550
Speaker 1: Romans 3:24.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.550
Speaker 1: I want to read verse 25, Romans 3:25.

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:06.110
Speaker 1: It says, whom God set forth.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:11.770
Speaker 1: This is talking about Jesus, whom God set forth as propitiation by his blood.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:16.930
Speaker 1: So his blood is the means through which we had this appeasement.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:17.410
Speaker 1: All right?

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:30.690
Speaker 1: Propitiation by his blood through faith to demonstrate his righteousness because of the forbearance of God, he has passed over the sins which was previously committed.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:42.260
Speaker 1: First John, chapter 2, verse 2, says, and he himself is the propitiation for our sins, and not for us only, but for the sins of the world.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:53.660
Speaker 1: Listen, one hand, this is good theological stuff, something that you can know, terminology that you can become familiar with, you know, from now onward, you know.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:56.180
Speaker 1: But also this is something personal and powerful.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.140
Speaker 1: This is what Christ has done for you know?

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:07.000
Speaker 1: Now you stand without guilt, unblamable in the sight of God because of the propitiation that is in Christ Jesus.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:08.600
Speaker 1: Glory to his name.
